0:{"buildId":"","data":[{"rsc":["$","$1","c",{"children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"min-h-screen ","style":{"backgroundColor":"hsl(var(--content-bg))"},"children":[["$","script",null,{"type":"application/ld+json","dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"{\"@context\":\"https://schema.org\",\"@type\":\"BreadcrumbList\",\"itemListElement\":[{\"@type\":\"ListItem\",\"position\":1,\"name\":\"Home\",\"item\":\"3e181358e45b6bbedd4f.htc\"},{\"@type\":\"ListItem\",\"position\":2,\"name\":\"Rules\",\"item\":\"5f3060d775e75b6b30ea.htc\"}]}"}}],["$","nav",null,{"aria-label":"Breadcrumb","className":"max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 pt-6","children":["$","ol",null,{"className":"flex items-center space-x-2 text-sm","style":{"color":"hsl(var(--content-fg-muted))"},"children":[["$","li","/",{"className":"flex items-center","children":[false,["$","$L2",null,{"href":"/","className":"transition-colors hover:opacity-80","style":{"color":"hsl(var(--content-fg))"},"children":"Home"}]]}],["$","li","402816ab8c24213bf3c6.htc",{"className":"flex items-center","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mx-2","children":"/"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"font-medium","style":{"color":"hsl(var(--content-fg))"},"children":"Rules"}]]}]]}]}],["$","article",null,{"className":"max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-8","children":[["$","h1",null,{"className":"text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold mb-8","style":{"color":"hsl(var(--content-fg))"},"children":"Klondike Solitaire Rules: How This Game Plays Turn 1 & Turn 3"}],"$undefined","$undefined",["$","div",null,{"className":"prose prose-lg max-w-none","children":[["$","section","0",{"className":"mb-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-2xl font-bold mb-4","style":{"color":"hsl(var(--content-fg))"},"children":"What is Klondike Solitaire?"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"whitespace-pre-line leading-relaxed","style":{"color":"hsl(var(--content-fg-muted))"},"children":"Klondike is the most famous solitaire card game in the world. When most people say \"Solitaire,\" they mean Klondike â€” a single-player game beloved for its balance of simplicity, strategy, and luck.\n\nThis page covers the rules as they play on this site. For the full history and background of the game, see the linked rules-hub guide at the bottom of the page."}]]}],["$","section","1",{"className":"mb-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-2xl font-bold mb-4","style":{"color":"hsl(var(--content-fg))"},"children":"Game Layout"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"whitespace-pre-line leading-relaxed","style":{"color":"hsl(var(--content-fg-muted))"},"children":"Klondike uses four distinct areas:\n\nTableau: The main playing area with 7 columns of cards. This is where most of the game takes place. You rearrange cards between columns to uncover hidden cards and build sequences.\n\nStock Pile: The remaining cards after dealing the tableau. You draw from the stock when you need new cards to play with.\n\nWaste Pile: Cards drawn from the stock are placed here face-up. Only the top card of the waste pile is available to play.\n\nFoundations: Four empty spaces (usually in the top right) where you build your final piles. Each foundation is for one suit, built from Ace up to King. Getting all 52 cards onto the foundations is how you win."}]]}],["$","section","2",{"className":"mb-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-2xl font-bold mb-4","style":{"color":"hsl(var(--content-fg))"},"children":"Setting Up"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"whitespace-pre-line leading-relaxed","style":{"color":"hsl(var(--content-fg-muted))"},"children":"Dealing the Klondike tableau follows a specific pattern:\n\nColumn 1 (far left): 1 card, face-up.\nColumn 2: 2 cards â€” 1 face-down, 1 face-up on top.\nColumn 3: 3 cards â€” 2 face-down, 1 face-up on top.\nColumn 4: 4 cards â€” 3 face-down, 1 face-up on top.\nColumn 5: 5 cards â€” 4 face-down, 1 face-up on top.\nColumn 6: 6 cards â€” 5 face-down, 1 face-up on top.\nColumn 7 (far right): 7 cards â€” 6 face-down, 1 face-up on top.\n\nThis uses 28 cards total (1+2+3+4+5+6+7 = 28). The remaining 24 cards form the stock pile, placed face-down.\n\nOnly the top card of each column is face-up and playable. The face-down cards create the mystery and challenge â€” you must uncover them by removing the cards on top."}]]}],"$L3","$L4","$L5","$L6","$L7","$L8","$L9","$La","$Lb","$Lc"]}],"$Ld","$Le"]}],"$Lf"]}],null,"$L10"]}],"isPartial":"$@11","staleTime":"$12","varyParams":null},{"rsc":"$L13","isPartial":"$@14","staleTime":"$12","varyParams":null},{"rsc":"$L15","isPartial":"$@16","staleTime":"$12","varyParams":"$17"}],"isUpgradeableISRFallback":false,"a":"$@18","rootVaryParams":null,"needsRuntimeRequest":"$@19"}
