3:["$","section","3",{"className":"mb-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-2xl font-bold mb-4","style":{"color":"hsl(var(--content-fg))"},"children":"Objective"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"whitespace-pre-line leading-relaxed","style":{"color":"hsl(var(--content-fg-muted))"},"children":"The goal of Klondike is to build four foundation piles, one for each suit, in ascending order from Ace to King:\n\nAce, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10, Jack, Queen, King\n\nYou must build each suit separately â€” all hearts go on one foundation, all diamonds on another, all clubs on the third, and all spades on the fourth.\n\nThe game is won when all 52 cards have been moved to the four foundation piles. This requires uncovering all face-down tableau cards, playing through the stock pile, and organizing cards in the correct order."}]]}]
4:["$","section","4",{"className":"mb-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-2xl font-bold mb-4","style":{"color":"hsl(var(--content-fg))"},"children":"Playing from the Stock"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"whitespace-pre-line leading-relaxed","style":{"color":"hsl(var(--content-fg-muted))"},"children":"When you have no moves available on the tableau, you can draw cards from the stock pile.\n\nIn Draw 1 mode, you flip one card at a time from the stock to the waste pile. This gives you access to every card in the stock in order.\n\nIn Draw 3 mode, you flip three cards at a time. Only the top card of each set of three is playable. This means you can only access every third card unless you cycle through the entire stock multiple times.\n\nWhen the stock pile runs out, you can flip the waste pile back over to form a new stock pile and continue drawing. There is no limit to how many times you can cycle through the stock in standard Klondike rules.\n\nThe top card of the waste pile can be played to any valid tableau column or directly to a foundation."}]]}]
5:["$","section","5",{"className":"mb-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-2xl font-bold mb-4","style":{"color":"hsl(var(--content-fg))"},"children":"Building the Tableau"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"whitespace-pre-line leading-relaxed","style":{"color":"hsl(var(--content-fg-muted))"},"children":"The core of Klondike gameplay is moving cards between tableau columns. The building rules are:\n\nDescending Rank: Each card placed on a column must be one rank lower than the card it is placed on. A 9 goes on a 10, a Jack goes on a Queen, etc.\n\nAlternating Colors: Cards must alternate between red (hearts, diamonds) and black (clubs, spades). A red 7 goes on a black 8. A black Jack goes on a red Queen.\n\nMoving Sequences: Unlike FreeCell, you can move an entire face-up sequence of properly ordered cards from one column to another. For example, if a column has \"black 8, red 7, black 6\" you can move all three cards together onto a red 9.\n\nThese rules create cascading sequences of alternating colors. A well-organized column might look like: red King, black Queen, red Jack, black 10, red 9..."}]]}]
6:["$","section","6",{"className":"mb-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-2xl font-bold mb-4","style":{"color":"hsl(var(--content-fg))"},"children":"Moving Kings"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"whitespace-pre-line leading-relaxed","style":{"color":"hsl(var(--content-fg-muted))"},"children":"Kings have a special role in Klondike: they are the only cards that can be placed in an empty tableau column.\n\nWhen a tableau column becomes empty (all cards removed), only a King or a sequence starting with a King can be placed there. This rule is different from FreeCell where any card can fill an empty column.\n\nThis makes King placement a critical strategic decision. Choosing which King to place in an empty column can determine whether you win or lose. Ideally, you want to place a King that allows you to build a long sequence and uncover face-down cards in other columns.\n\nSince there are only 4 Kings and 7 columns, creating empty columns is less useful in Klondike than in FreeCell â€” you need Kings to fill them productively."}]]}]
7:["$","section","7",{"className":"mb-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-2xl font-bold mb-4","style":{"color":"hsl(var(--content-fg))"},"children":"Turning Over Cards"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"whitespace-pre-line leading-relaxed","style":{"color":"hsl(var(--content-fg-muted))"},"children":"Whenever you remove the last face-up card from a tableau column, the top face-down card is automatically flipped face-up. This is one of the most satisfying moments in Klondike â€” revealing a hidden card and discovering new possibilities.\n\nUncovering face-down cards is essential to winning. Each face-down card flipped is progress, and each one might be the Ace, King, or sequence card you need to advance your game.\n\nThe 21 face-down cards in the initial deal (0+1+2+3+4+5+6 = 21 face-down cards) represent the unknown element that makes each Klondike game unique. Your ability to strategically uncover these cards determines your success."}]]}]
8:["$","section","8",{"className":"mb-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-2xl font-bold mb-4","style":{"color":"hsl(var(--content-fg))"},"children":"Winning the Game"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"whitespace-pre-line leading-relaxed","style":{"color":"hsl(var(--content-fg-muted))"},"children":"You win Klondike when all 52 cards are on the four foundation piles, with each suit built from Ace to King.\n\nIn practice, the game often ends with a cascade: once you have uncovered all face-down cards and organized the tableau, the remaining cards flow to the foundations in rapid succession.\n\nNot every Klondike deal is winnable. With perfect play (omniscient strategy), approximately 79-82% of deals are solvable. In practice, most players win 15-30% of their games, with expert players reaching 40-50% win rates.\n\nYou lose when no more moves are possible: no tableau moves, no stock cards to draw, and no way to play the waste pile card. Some positions are clearly lost, while others require careful analysis to determine if any winning path remains."}]]}]
1a:T420,The two main Klondike modes offer very different experiences:

Draw 1 (Turn 1): You flip one card at a time from the stock. This gives you access to every stock card in sequence and makes the game significantly easier. Estimated win rate with perfect play: approximately 82%. This mode is recommended for beginners and casual players.

Draw 3 (Turn 3): You flip three cards at a time, and only the top card is playable. This means two-thirds of the stock cards are initially inaccessible, requiring multiple passes through the stock to reach them. Estimated win rate with perfect play: approximately 79%. This mode adds a significant strategic layer.

Draw 3 strategy differs from Draw 1 in several ways:

You must plan ahead when cycling through the stock, knowing which cards are coming.
Sometimes you play a less optimal card to shift the stock alignment and reveal a card you need.
Multiple stock passes are usually necessary, so conservation of moves matters more.

Choose Draw 1 for a relaxing experience. Choose Draw 3 for a more challenging puzzle.9:["$","section","9",{"className":"mb-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-2xl font-bold mb-4","style":{"color":"hsl(var(--content-fg))"},"children":"Draw 1 vs Draw 3"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"whitespace-pre-line leading-relaxed","style":{"color":"hsl(var(--content-fg-muted))"},"children":"$1a"}]]}]
a:["$","section","10",{"className":"mb-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-2xl font-bold mb-4","style":{"color":"hsl(var(--content-fg))"},"children":"Difficulty settings on this site"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"whitespace-pre-line leading-relaxed","style":{"color":"hsl(var(--content-fg-muted))"},"children":"The difficulty selector on the game table maps directly to the classic draw modes and pass limits:\n\nEasy: Draw 1 with unlimited passes through the stock. Flip one card at a time and recycle the waste as many times as you like â€” the most forgiving way to play and the best place to learn.\n\nMedium: Draw 1 with three passes through the stock. You still flip one card at a time, but you may only cycle through the stock three times, so every draw counts a little more.\n\nHard (Turn 3): Draw 3 with three passes through the stock. Cards come off the stock three at a time and only the top one is playable, with three passes allowed â€” the traditional tournament-style challenge."}]]}]
b:["$","section","11",{"className":"mb-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-2xl font-bold mb-4","style":{"color":"hsl(var(--content-fg))"},"children":"Controls"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"whitespace-pre-line leading-relaxed","style":{"color":"hsl(var(--content-fg-muted))"},"children":"The game is built for both mouse and touch:\n\nMove a card by clicking it and then clicking a valid destination, or by dragging it there directly.\n\nDouble-click or double-tap a card to send it straight to its foundation when a legal foundation move exists.\n\nClick the stock pile to draw the next card or group of cards to the waste.\n\nWhen the stock is empty, click the empty stock space to redeal the waste back into the stock â€” as long as you still have a pass remaining at your difficulty."}]]}]
c:["$","section","12",{"className":"mb-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-2xl font-bold mb-4","style":{"color":"hsl(var(--content-fg))"},"children":"Undo, hints, auto-finish, and stats"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"whitespace-pre-line leading-relaxed","style":{"color":"hsl(var(--content-fg-muted))"},"children":"Every deal comes with a full set of assists:\n\nUnlimited undo and redo let you rewind a line of play and try a different one, all the way back to the opening move.\n\nThe hint button highlights an available move when you are stuck.\n\nAuto-finish appears once the board is safe â€” when every remaining card can reach the foundations â€” and clears the rest for you in one click.\n\nA timer, move counter, and win statistics track your progress and persist in your browser between sessions, so your record carries over the next time you play."}]]}]
